{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"write-exploit-regression-test","kind":"prompt","title":"Write a regression test for a fixed vulnerability","description":"Turns a fixed vulnerability into an automated test that replays the attack safely and fails if the flaw ever returns, checking the test fails on the vulnerable code first.","category":"security","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["verify"],"role":["software-engineer","security-engineer","qa-engineer"],"stack":[],"requires":["repo-read","file-write","shell","git"],"inputs":["diff","ticket","repo","text"],"output":["tests"],"risk":"runs-commands","invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"autonomous","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["regression","security-testing","vulnerability-fix"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["triage-vulnerability-report","add-regression-test","write-semgrep-rule","review-pr-for-security"],"personas":["security-auditor"]},"args":[{"name":"vulnerability","description":"The vulnerability report or advisory, and the fix commit, PR or diff.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"stack","description":"Language and test framework. Leave empty to detect it from the repo.","type":"string"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Attack summary","Tests added","Proof the tests catch the flaw","Verification","Gaps"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nSecurity fixes regress more often than ordinary bugs because the vulnerable shape is easy to reintroduce: a new endpoint copies the old pattern, a refactor drops a check, a dependency downgrade brings the flaw back. A useful security regression test attacks the property, not the patch: it asserts \"user B cannot read user A's invoice\" or \"a `../` path is rejected\", so it still fails if the check moves. It goes through the real entry point (HTTP route, resolver, job handler) rather than calling the fixed helper directly, because the original bug was usually a missing call to the helper. It covers bypass variants the attacker would try next (encoding, case, alternate parameter location), uses harmless payloads, and is proven to fail on the vulnerable code before it is trusted. Variant tests for sibling endpoints with the same shape catch the copy-paste regressions.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nWrite regression tests for this fixed vulnerability:\n{{vulnerability}}\n{{#stack}}Stack: {{stack}}{{/stack}}\n\n1. Read the report and the fix. State the vulnerable property in one sentence (who can do what they should not, through which entry point).\n2. Read the existing test setup (fixtures, factories, authenticated clients) and follow its conventions.\n3. Write the main test through the real entry point: set up the attacker and victim (or the hostile input), send the original attack with a harmless payload, and assert the safe outcome (status code, no data leaked, no side effect, input rejected).\n4. Add variant tests for the bypasses an attacker would try next for this flaw class, and the same attack against sibling endpoints or handlers with the same shape.\n5. Add a positive test showing the legitimate use still works, so the fix cannot be \"kept\" by breaking the feature.\n6. Prove the tests catch the flaw: check out the commit before the fix (or revert the fix in a scratch working copy), run the new tests and confirm they fail, then restore the fixed code and confirm they pass. Ask before using any git command that would discard local changes.\n7. Name the tests clearly (for example `test_user_cannot_download_other_users_invoice`) and reference the advisory or ticket id in a comment, without exploit details beyond what the test needs.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Use harmless payloads only; no payloads that delete data, open shells or contact external hosts.\n- Test the security property, not the internal helper that implements the fix.\n- Never mark tests as skipped or flaky to get a green run.\n- Do not include secrets, real customer data or reporter personal details in test names or fixtures.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Fix the behaviour, not the test. Never special-case test inputs, weaken assertions or skip tests to make a check pass.\n- If a test looks wrong, explain why and ask before changing it.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Attack summary\nThe vulnerable property, entry point and flaw class in two or three sentences.\n## Tests added\nOne line per test: name, what it attacks, expected safe result.\n## Proof the tests catch the flaw\nHow the vulnerable version was run and which tests failed on it.\n## Verification\nCommands run and their actual results on the fixed code.\n## Gaps\nVariants not covered and why.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
